 "module": "13470",
 "chapter": "10",
 "title": "Hybrid Orbitals Account for Molecular Shape",
 "short": "Hybrid orbitals",
 "lead": "Valence bond theory builds a bond from one orbital on each atom. Mixing an atom's s and p orbitals into hybrids gives it equivalent orbitals aimed in new directions, which is how the theory reaches the shapes of BeH₂, BH₃ and CH₄.",
 "objectives": [
  "Introduce hybrid orbital to explain non-linear molecular structure"
 ],
 "summary_html": "<p>The shape and bonding valecies of polyatomic molecules can be accounted for by <span data-concept=\"hybrid-orbital\">hybrid orbitals</span>. Molecular orbitals are formed from linear combinations of atomic orbitals which are similar in energy. These atomic orbitals could come from different atoms, or from the same atom. For example, the 2 sand 2patomic orbitals<sup class=\"fn\" data-note=\"c-summary\"></sup> are very close energetically. When a linear combo of more than one atomic orbital from the <strong>same atom</strong> is formed, we have a <span data-concept=\"hybrid-orbital\">hybrid orbital</span>.</p>",
 "notes": "By Mark Tuckerman, shared under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0; the book credits none of its figures.",
