 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "ai1",
   "source_id": "Finally, we consider the case of methane",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "sp3",
   "prompt": "<p>Take the first two of methane's <em>sp</em><sup>3</sup> hybrids in Equation 10.1.25, $\\chi_1 = \\frac{1}{2}[\\psi_{2s}+\\psi_{2p_x}+\\psi_{2p_y}+\\psi_{2p_z}]$ and $\\chi_2 = \\frac{1}{2}[\\psi_{2s}-\\psi_{2p_x}-\\psi_{2p_y}+\\psi_{2p_z}]$, with orthonormal atomic orbitals. (a) What is $\\int |\\chi_1|^2 dV$? (b) What is $\\int \\chi_1^*\\chi_2\\, dV$? (c) Each hybrid points along the direction given by its $p_x$, $p_y$, $p_z$ coefficients. What angle, in degrees, lies between the directions of $\\chi_1$ and $\\chi_2$?</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 1,
      "tol": 0.001,
      "hint": "Only the squares of the coefficients survive the integral."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 0,
      "tol": 0.001,
      "hint": "Multiply the coefficients of the same orbital in the two hybrids and add."
     },
     {
      "part": "c",
      "value": 109.47,
      "unit": "°",
      "tol": 0.002,
      "hint": "The directions are (1, 1, 1) and (−1, −1, 1); use the dot product."
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>(a) The atomic orbitals are orthonormal, so the cross terms vanish and $\\int|\\chi_1|^2 dV = \\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 \\times 4 = 1$.</p><p>(b) Only products of the same orbital survive: $\\int\\chi_1^*\\chi_2\\,dV = \\tfrac{1}{4}\\left[(1)(1) + (1)(-1) + (1)(-1) + (1)(1)\\right] = 0$. The two hybrids are orthogonal, as Equation 10.1.10 asks of any pair.</p><p>(c) $\\chi_1$ points along (1, 1, 1) and $\\chi_2$ along (−1, −1, 1). Then $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{(1)(-1) + (1)(-1) + (1)(1)}{\\sqrt{3}\\,\\sqrt{3}} = -\\tfrac{1}{3}$, so $\\theta = 109.47^\\circ$, the tetrahedral angle of Figure 10.1.8.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
